From 2835895dd50f3de71329756fd6fd0892f1e183a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 00:52:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 04/40] phy: samsung: add the UFS PHY driver for Exynos9820

Storage on this SoC sits behind a PHY that has to be calibrated at four
points of the link bring-up, and upstream has nothing for it: drivers/phy
holds one Exynos driver, for USB, and no part of the Samsung UFS PHY at
all.  So both halves arrive here, the part every SoC of the family shares
and the calibration data of this one.

The Linux driver drivers/phy/samsung/phy-samsung-ufs.c makes the right
split between those two halves and this follows its shape, but two things
had to change.  Linux keeps the calibration point in a state machine inside
the PHY and advances it from a .calibrate operation; the PHY uclass here
has no such operation, so the point travels the other way, from the host
controller into .configure, which also takes the guesswork out of which
point a call is for. And Linux runs the per SoC waits after the whole table
has been written, while the vendor's tables put those waits in the middle
of a sequence; here a wait is an entry of the table, so the hardware sees
it where the vendor put it.

Every value comes from the vendor's own calibration layer for this SoC,
drivers/scsi/ufs/ufs-cal-9820.c, and each line names the line it came from.
Which of its tables apply is decided by three properties the board's device
tree carries: evt-ver = [01] takes the tables for the second revision of
the silicon, ufs,pmd-attr-gear = [03] takes the gear 3 variant of the post
link table, and brd-for-cal = [01] is BRD_ALL, which drops the lines meant
for the emulator.  The vendor's two high speed series turn out to be
identical on this SoC, so one table serves both and the series only decides
what .set_mode records.

Two details of that file are worth stating, because the obvious reading of
either gives a driver that writes to the wrong place and says nothing about
it.  The distance between the per lane register banks is 0x400 bytes, which
is what the 9820 copy of PHY_PMA_TRSV_ADDR says; the copy in ufs-exynos.h
beside it says 0x140 and is dead, nothing in that tree calls it.  And the
wait for the receiver to lock polls forty microseconds apart, not four
hundred: ufs30_cal_wait_cdr_lock reads every delay2_us = 40 and waits
delay_us = 1 before it takes the clock recovery block down and back up.

The wait for the PHY's own calibration is advisory on purpose.  The vendor
leaves that polling loop with a success return even when the bit never
appears, so treating it as fatal would refuse to bring up storage over
something the hardware itself is not asked to guarantee.  The timeout is
reported instead, and a first bring-up on real hardware should watch for
the message.

The PMU driver gains this SoC's compatible, because the isolation control
the PHY needs lives behind that syscon and nothing else binds the node.

Hibernation is left out on purpose.  The vendor has tables for entering and
leaving it, but a boot loader reads storage and hands over; it never asks
the link to hibernate.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/phy/Kconfig              |  18 ++
 drivers/phy/Makefile             |   2 +
 drivers/phy/phy-exynos9820-ufs.c | 285 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/phy/phy-samsung-ufs.c    | 303 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/phy/phy-samsung-ufs.h    | 193 ++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-pmu.c |   3 +
 include/samsung-ufs-phy.h        |  52 ++++++
 7 files changed, 856 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/phy/phy-exynos9820-ufs.c
 create mode 100644 drivers/phy/phy-samsung-ufs.c
 create mode 100644 drivers/phy/phy-samsung-ufs.h
 create mode 100644 include/samsung-ufs-phy.h

